Z||000000||b lo 0||07-26-2001||08:59 PM||||Still plagued by the fuel pump fuse problem.  The fuse pops whenever I press the gas down when the engine is hot.  New fuel pump, filter, ignition module, fuel pressure sensor, and all pump-related wiring.  I think it may be the coil.  Code 12 has been verified in the computer.  Please help!!!<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">      <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||BC-GT||07-26-2001||09:55 PM||||Believe it shares a circuit with your oil pressure switch, you might want to look there.<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||West Coast Fiero||07-26-2001||10:23 PM||||as simple as my answer sounds I would check to see if there is a short curcuit in one if the wires - though this may not cure itr||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||07-26-2001||10:45 PM||||Gotta be a short somewhere.  Mine was in the large wiring harness on the opposite side of the oil sending unit/fuel pump wire circuit.   When the 3.1 was installed the wire harness went over the heat shield instead of behind it. Every time the engine moved, the heat shield, which had melted through the wire, would then short the wire, blowing the fuse. Try re wrapping all the wire harnesses with some insulated heat resistant tape.  I went through dozens of fuel pump fuses, two fuel pumps and four tows for repairs before the car ended up at Twin Lakes Fiero and Bob found the wire routing problem, and fixed it.<P>Intermittant short to ground some where.   Big PITA to locate and fix.||||reg||1
Z||000004||b lo 0||07-27-2001||12:10 AM||||Sorry, I replaced the oil pressure sensor.  Typo in original post.  BTW, Old Lar, I have replaced EVERY BIT of wiring that has ANYTHING to do with the fuel pump.  Yeah, PITA, you're tellin' me.  BTW, it is all wrapped in industrial heat shrink AND heat reflective tape.  Am I completely insane for thinking it's the coil?  The motor won't rev past about 5k also.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||07-27-2001||08:15 AM||||Yes, you are completely insane thinking it's the coil, if you mean ignition coil is blowing fuel pump fuse.  Power to fuel pump is supplied from an independent 10A fuse.  That says intermittent short hiding somewhere, or faulty fuel pump; I know you said fuel pump was new, and "all" pump-related wiring was replaced.<P>Was the new wiring carefully routed out of harm's way?.  What about the wiring from the fuse box, back to the fuel pump relay?  That's about all there is left to short out!  <P>As far as no rev &gt;5000rpm goes, guess it could be the coil - very easy to swap and try, and never hurts having another coil around.     <P>||||reg||4
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||07-27-2001||11:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by b lo 0:<BR><B>The fuse pops whenever I press the gas down when the engine is hot.    Please help!!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Are you talking about depressing the pedal to the floor? <BR>Look for interference with the wiring behind the pedal, chaffing in the tunnel (under console), wire rub in the engine compartment around the throttle body and cable run.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||b lo 0||07-27-2001||04:50 PM||||Usually it's only when the engine is hot and I press the gas down more than halfway from a dead stop.  I know the pump is good, checked it with the meter (old and new).  The reason my dad and I think it's the coil (he's a mechanic and electronics tech with 33 years experience) is because the signal for the fuel pump goes thru the ICM and coil before it goes to the computer and finally the pump (check the Hayne's schematics for the fuel pump circuit) and also the code 12 has been verified as a true fault code and not just the check code.  Also, the engine will not rev past around 5k rpm, which seems like a loss of high-rpm spark voltage (which comes from, that's right, the coil.)  Every bit of wiring has been replaced, and double-checked.  Potential chafe points have been covered with split heater hose (I know better, my old Nissan was rendered useless after a short over the alternator bracket fried the computer).  I've heard the "check the wiring" bit before (a WHOLE lot) and I need new ideas.  I do appreciate your help, though guys!!  (really I do)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jake_Dragon||07-27-2001||05:49 PM||||Could you have the wrong amp fuse?||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||07-27-2001||06:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by b lo 0:<BR><B>...the signal for the fuel pump goes thru the ICM and coil before it goes to the computer and finally the pump (check the Hayne's schematics for the fuel pump circuit) and also the code 12 has been verified as a true fault code and not just the check code...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Now I'm really confused (it doesn't take much  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).  I don't know what an ICM is, I assume you mean ECM (the computer)?.  And when you say the <B>signal</B> for the fuel pump, do you mean one that powers the fuel pump relay's coil (dark green+ white wire)?  I don't know where that circuit is fused - but might be an ECM fuse.   <P>My Haynes says the fuel pump fuse (the one that actually runs the pump directly) come through a 10-amp fuse, which is fed by a fusible link "A."  Is <B>that</B> the one that keeps blowing, the 10-amp fuse in the fuse block, labeled "Fuel Pump?"  <P>Not trying to be a pain; just trying to understand  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000010||johnt671||07-27-2001||07:35 PM||||I had a problem with my fuel pump( a bit diff. than yours) and it turned out to be the ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||07-27-2001||08:18 PM||||Slow down and think about this a minute....<P>What heppens when the gas pedal is pushed?<P>Obviously the car goes faster and the pedal moves....<P>Someone mentioned the oil pressure switch... That's on the right track but it wasn't likely the switch itself.<P>When the pedal is pushed the <I>engine</I> moves on it's mounts. This is probably tugging on some wires and has finally dug a hole into one and now it's shorting but only under conditions that let the motor move beyond X amount.<P>Also, the fuel tank can shift some on it's mounts. There may be damage that was missed when the fuel pump was replaced. As you slow down the tank would tend to shift forward then slide back when you take off. <P>You'll need to physically inspect the harness to find where the engine or what ever is diggin into it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||b lo 0||07-28-2001||12:56 AM||||By ICM I mean ignition control module (where the plugs go on the side of the distributor).  I've had the tank out twice and the straps looked good, and the wiring that goes harness to pump (along tank) was just replaced, including the 3 pin plug there.  I wll check the oil press switch wiring, but I'm pretty sure it's OK (replaced switch to get gauge working).  As far as wrong fuse, book said 10 amp (yes, labeled "fuel pump"), and I blew about 20-30 of those before I finally used an old one to wire in a 10 amp 12v circuit breaker (just switch it back on-also makes great anti-theft switch).  As soon as I have denero I will replace the coil, none as of yet (anybody wanna give me one?).  Ogre, I respect your opinion on all things technical, so don't take this wrong, but 99% of the harness has been physically inspected and/or replaced.  Thanks for the help!<P>PS-by signal, I mean what tells the computer to run the pump when the engine is running.||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||07-28-2001||01:24 PM||||The computer simply knows that the key is on and starts the pump.<P>The computer can't blow the pump fuse. You'd likely fry the ECM if that happened. The ECM wire only goes to the FP relay coil. The other side of the coil goes to ground. The 2 circuits are supposed to be fully isolated from each other.<P>A damaged relay could posibly do it, but unlikely. The relay would most likey blow the circuit as soon as you hit the key.||||reg||1
Z||000014||maryjane||07-28-2001||07:40 PM||||First of all, I'm venturing where I've not a lot of 'on hands' expierence. If I'm going astray, SOMEBODY jump in and say so,quickly.<BR>Have you checked fuel pressure? Can a restriction in either the inlet or pressure side of the fuel sys cause overload on the pump=high current draw, blowing the fuse? If so, it might also cause fuel starvation so the eng cannot reach full rpm.<BR>Next idea. isolate the fp elec system into sections. Disconnect the wire on the fp relay,"d" terminal I think. Make sure you have 12 V to the oil press switch. If not you may have to run a FUSED jumper to input side of the switch. Use a source other than the wire from the fuel pump fuse.This should effectively take fp relay,ECM, and their wiring out of the picture. It may be hard to start, using the oil press switch to trigger the fuel pump, but it should start after reaching 4psi oil press. If the problem goes away, the prob lies in the fp relay,associated 'upstream wiring' or ECM.(I also do not believe the ECM itself can blow a 10 amp fuse without damaging the ECM)<BR>I offer these ideas as troubleshooting aides only, not intended to use as everyday cures.<BR>Anybody want to add or correct? Ogre?<BR>Good luck<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000015||b lo 0||07-29-2001||04:28 PM||||Thanks for the suggestions.  Ogre, I don't think it's the relay, that was actually the first thing I checked.  Is my car just insane?  'Cause it's makin me that way.  I'll double check the oil press. switch wiring.  Maryjane, thanks for the suggestion, but I need to fix it, not jury rig it.  It's semi-driveable right now.  Any ideas other than wiring?  'Cause I already pretty much ruled that out, and that's all you say it could be, Ogre.  You're a Fiero mechanical genius, and I've only been working on mine for a year.  One more tidbit-when checking for spark, the spark is great with the distributor is removed and a ground clip is attached, but once it's in the engine the spark sucks, even with the ground clip still attached.  The distributor has been completely rebuilt (new bobbin coil, ECM, cap & rotor, and wires).  Thanks guys!  Especially Ogre, you always seem to waste your time on me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!||||reg||1
Z||000016||maryjane||07-29-2001||08:36 PM||||I wasn't suggesting you jury rig it, just to try isolating parts of the system to narrow the problem down a little. Wish you luck, sounds like a exorcist may be needed.||||reg||1
